I realized that we're having a concept of vehicle types that's not really being used and also isn't documented, e.g. a `type` request option which can take values of  `four_wheel_drive` etc but for car that can even be `motorcycle` which seems weird. Those travel types are hardly ever used anywhere and with the exception of `bicycle_type` and it's not even documented that the others exist.

#### Car

```c++
  const std::string& type = costing_options.transport_type();
  if (type == "motorcycle") {
    type_ = VehicleType::kMotorcycle;
    surface_factor_ = 1.0f;
  } else if (type == "bus") {
    type_ = VehicleType::kBus;
  } else if (type == "tractor_trailer") {
    type_ = VehicleType::kTractorTrailer;
  } else if (type == "four_wheel_drive") {
    type_ = VehicleType::kFourWheelDrive;
    surface_factor_ = 0.0f;
  } else {
    type_ = VehicleType::kCar;
  }
  ```

Here I'd like to comment out the whole thing, and just do `type_ = VehicleType::kCar`. `kTractorTrailer` is confusingly our truck type as well, and `kMotorcycle` is our motorcycle type, both shouldn't be available in AutoCosting. The `kFourWheelDrive` does do a tiny little bit, but that's also a bit too little to be called 4WD maybe?

##### Pedestrian

```c++
  const std::string& type = costing_options.transport_type();
  if (type == "wheelchair") {
    type_ = PedestrianType::kWheelchair;
  } else if (type == "segway") {
    type_ = PedestrianType::kSegway;
  } else {
    type_ = PedestrianType::kFoot;
  }
``` 

Again, nothing here is actually used other than `kFoot`. Also wheelchair not, other than for the access mask. For transit we expose a `wheelchair` bool request parameter, i.e. not even transit does anything with this wheelchair `transport_type`. A lot more should be done and I'd rather remove it than document it and have people use it, as it's really not remotely usable for any person in a wheelchair. Segway haha I'd also dump that;)

What do you think?

